The attached image illustrates the dendrimer-like structure of the Nano-11 alpha-D-glucan nanoparticles. Each sphere depicts a central core from which highly branched structures radiate outwards, forming a dense network. This visual representation aligns with the PDF's description of Nano-11 as "dendrimer-like" and possessing a "high molecular density which increases from the center to the surface of the particle" (J Control Release, Section 3.1). This unique, highly branched architecture, a result of the lack of a debranching enzyme in the sweet corn source, is crucial for its function as a vaccine adjuvant, potentially contributing to its high adsorptive capacity for antigens and efficient uptake by dendritic cells.